            <p>

                The fastest way to get started is the native installer &mdash; a single command with no dependencies, and it keeps Claude Code updated automatically.
                <ul>
                    <li>macOS / Linux / WSL: <code>curl -fsSL https://claude.ai/install.sh | bash</code></li>
                    <li>Windows (PowerShell): <code>irm https://claude.ai/install.ps1 | iex</code></li>
                    <li>Go to your project: <code>cd your-project</code></li>
                    <li>Launch it: <code>claude</code></li>
                </ul>
                Homebrew (<code>brew install --cask claude-code</code>) and WinGet are also supported. Installing with npm (<code>npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code</code>, requires Node.js 22 or newer) still works and installs the same native binary.
                On first launch, Claude Code walks you through authentication &mdash; you can log in with a Claude account (Pro/Max plan) or an Anthropic API key. After that, just describe what you want in plain language, for example: <em>"explain the auth flow"</em> or <em>"add a dark mode toggle and run the tests"</em>.
                A few handy startup flags:
                <ul>
                    <li><code>claude</code> &mdash; start an interactive session</li>
                    <li><code>claude "your prompt"</code> &mdash; run a one-off task</li>
                    <li><code>claude -c</code> &mdash; continue the most recent conversation</li>
                    <li><code>claude --help</code> &mdash; list all options</li>
                </ul>

            </p>

            <h3>Everyday Use</h3>
            <p>

                Once installed and authenticated, most of the time you simply type what you want in natural language and Claude Code does the work &mdash; reading files, editing code, running commands, and asking for permission before anything risky. Alongside plain prompts, there are built-in slash commands that control the session:
                <ul>
                    <li><code>/help</code> &mdash; show available commands</li>
                    <li><code>/clear</code> &mdash; start a fresh conversation and reset context</li>
                    <li><code>/init</code> &mdash; generate a CLAUDE.md file documenting your project</li>
                    <li><code>/review</code> &mdash; review a pull request or your working changes</li>
                    <li><code>/config</code> &mdash; open settings (theme, model, and more)</li>
                    <li><code>/model</code> &mdash; switch the Claude model in use</li>
                </ul>
                Two tips that make a big difference:
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>CLAUDE.md</strong> &mdash; a file in your repo where you record project conventions, commands, and context. Claude Code reads it automatically so you don't have to repeat yourself.</li>
                    <li><strong>Plan mode</strong> &mdash; ask Claude to plan before it edits, so you can approve the approach on larger tasks before any code changes.</li>
                </ul>
                That covers the basics. Claude Code's real power comes from extending it, and there are three ways in: skills, MCP servers, and hooks &mdash; the story continues in <a href="#!skills">Extending Claude Code</a>.

            </p>
